W7 (#11): hierarchical sky130hd place & route of sigil_soc_top for the Caravel user area
Branch 11-sky130-hier; host vivado_rnd (r7i.4xlarge, 16 vCPU), ORFS image
openroad/orfs@sha256:63981d1ac8990a9bee8bf874c95ee2c1ee3fe7b99f57d459282ab8c31a8ed663,
<= 12 threads in total; 2026-10-22 (UTC). Config: flow/designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/
(top config.mk + sigil_cpu/ + sigil_crypto_top/ block configs). Same RTL and
sky130 configuration as the flat run (w7-sky130-pnr-2026-10-21.log):
SIGIL_SRAM_SKY130, 4 KiB direct-mapped L1s, 7 OpenRAM macros. RTL unchanged.

RESULT: DOES NOT FIT THE CARAVEL USER AREA; NO GDS. The hierarchical
flow works (blocks harden to LEF/LIB abstracts with met4 power pins,
the top synthesizes against block stubs, connects block power with
met5, places, runs CTS and detail-routes; top-level DRT converged to
1,009 violations by iteration 15 on provisional abstracts). The
crypto block detail-routes in its Caravel slot (converging, see
below). The CPU block does not: in its 7.42 mm^2 share of the
2920 x 3520 um die, global routing overflows on 45k-101k gcells at
77-78 % usage; it routes cleanly in global routing only at ~11.2 mm^2
(9.9 mm^2 still overflows). Whole SoC on sky130hd: ~14 mm^2, ~1.4x
the Caravel user area.

== Partition
  sigil_cpu         core + vector unit + FPU + L1 I$/D$ + bus arbiter + the
                    7 OpenRAM macros (macros stay with their logic: the
                    VRF and cache arrays have hundreds of pins each)
  sigil_crypto_top  SHA-256/512, Keccak, EC field unit, AXI4-Lite slave
                    (95 pins)
  top               reset synchronizer, fabric, CLINT, PLIC, UART, boot
                    ROM, crypto MMIO bridge (0.060 mm^2 of std cells)
  Block convention (Caravel-style macro): signals on met1-met4 only,
  power = met1 rails + met4 straps exported as VDD/VSS pins
  (block_pdn.tcl); the CPU block also exports the met4 straps over the
  OpenRAM vdd/gnd pin columns (sigil_cpu/post_pdn.tcl, 42 straps). The
  top's met5 stripes cross the blocks and via4 onto those pins
  (pdn.tcl). Abstract check: write_abstract_lef emits the met4 straps
  as PIN VDD/VSS and OBS li1-met4 only (met5 free over the blocks).

== Synthesis (per block, ORFS flat synthesis of each block)
  sigil_crypto_top  1,074,322 um^2 std cells (sequential 41.6 %), 100,776
                    cells; wall 249 s
  sigil_cpu         4,809,886 um^2 = 1,895,866 std cells + 2,914,020 macros,
                    209,935 cells; wall 2,904 s
  top glue          59,617 um^2; wall 22 s
  Note the split: the crypto engine is 36 % of the SoC's 2.97 mm^2 of std
  cells (Keccak f1600 + SHA-512 since W3).

== Top-level synthesis with hard blocks (why ORFS BLOCKS is not used)
  ORFS BLOCKS puts the block .libs into ADDITIONAL_LIBS for synthesis and
  blackboxes the block modules from their liberty view. yosys-slang gives
  the blackboxed u_cpu cell all of sigil_cpu's parameters, so
  `hierarchy -check` fails: "Module `sigil_cpu' ... does not have a
  parameter named 'C1_SIZE'" (same with SYNTH_BLACKBOXES). Working
  setup: synthesize the top from port-only stubs of the blocks
  (stubs/gen_stubs.sh, from the RTL headers) kept as blackboxes by
  slang --empty-blackboxes, without the block libs (SIGIL_TOP_SYNTH=1
  in the environment of the synth run), and strip the block-instance
  parameters in SYNTH_CANONICALIZE_TCL (stubs/strip_params.tcl):
  OpenSTA cannot parse "sigil_cpu #(...) u_cpu". Later stages read the
  block LEF/LIB/GDS through ADDITIONAL_LEFS/LIBS/GDS.

== Caravel budget (die 2920 x 3520 um = 10.28 mm^2)
  v1 floorplan: CPU 2900 x 2560 (7.42 mm^2) at the bottom, crypto
  2590 x 850 (2.20 mm^2) at the top, glue in a 90 um channel between
  them. v2 (current): 130 um glue column on the left die edge, CPU
  2770 x 2680 (7.42 mm^2) with pins on its left edge, crypto
  2770 x 780 (2.16 mm^2) above it with pins on its bottom edge (reached
  through a 40 um gap), SoC pins on the left die edge.
  Std-cell density this leaves: crypto 49 % of its core (55 % after
  placement repair/CTS); CPU 1.90 mm^2 in the 7.42 mm^2 block of which
  3.70 mm^2 is macros + 40 um halos (~51 %).

== Detailed-routing calibration (stock ORFS sky130hd/aes, same image, 1 thread)
  18,448 instances, 55 % utilization: DRT violations per iteration
  19,752 / 15,827 / 14,498 / 3,795 / 753 / 126 / 62 / 0 (iteration 7),
  route rc=0, wall 4,909 s. Iterations 0-2 rip up and re-route
  everything; the drop starts at iteration 3 (marker-driven rip-up).
  So iteration 0-2 counts alone do not show non-convergence: the
  flat run (1,036,832 / 842,148 / 833,572 at 30 % of iteration 2) was
  stopped before the first iteration that normally shows convergence,
  and its 0.80 iteration-1 ratio is the same as aes's.

== Crypto block experiments (all GRT 0 overflow unless stated)
  run    die (um)      util   GRT derating           GRT usage (m1/m2/m3/m4, total)        DRT iter 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 ...
  r1/s   2590 x 850    49 %   platform 0.2           76.0/64.2/58.6/37.2 %, 63.2 %         263,961 / 193,866 / 182,045 / 60,919 /
                                                                                                25,404 / 14,337 / 9,172 / 6,887 / 5,184 (iteration 8,
                                                                                                stopped for the v2 floorplan; 5,184 = 2,903 met1 + 633 met2
                                                                                                shorts, 1,191 met1 spacing ...)
  d42    2590 x 1000   42 %   platform 0.2           69.5/61.9/45.9/34.5 %, 57.1 %         233,428 (stopped)
  d35    2590 x 1190   35 %   platform 0.2           60.5/60.2/30.4/33.2 %, 49.9 %         186,146 (stopped)
  u30    2590 x 1400   30 %   0.2 all layers         71.9/48.7/57.1/12.7 %, 53.7 %         261,945 (stopped)
  m42    2590 x 1000   42 %   met1 0.7, others 0.2   97.4/90.4/94.2/68.6 %, 88.7 %         40,801 overflowed gcells (stopped)
  h30    2590 x 1400   30 %   met1 0.5, others 0.2   88.1/76.0/77.8/35.7 %, 73.2 %         15,856 overflowed gcells (stopped)
  Iteration-0 violations do not fall with area (264k at 49 %, 262k at
  30 %): they scale with instance count (~2 per instance; aes ~1.1),
  mostly met1 shorts (u30: 174,064 of 261,945). Reserving met1 for pin
  access did not help: FastRoute detours (global demand +18 %) instead
  of moving trunks to met3/met4, and overflows.

== CPU block experiments
  All with 40 um macro halos (RTLMP: 3.70 mm^2 of macros + halos).
  run    die (um)      mm^2   GRT derating          GRT result
  r1     2900 x 2560   7.42   0.3 (pre_grt.tcl)     usage 88.7 %, 214,046 overflowed gcells, 28.28 m wire
  s      2900 x 2560   7.42   0.2 (platform)        usage 76.9 % (m1 77.9, m2 78.3, m3 76.9, m4 71.2),
                                                    45,096 overflowed gcells, 28.42 m wire; GRT stage
                                                    (repair_design 20 min, repair_timing, antenna)
                                                    > 3 h; stopped before DRT
  v2     2770 x 2680   7.42   0.2                   usage 77.6 % (m1 80.6, m2 83.7, m3 72.5, m4 65.5),
                                                    100,783 overflowed gcells, 28.60 m wire (pins on
                                                    the left edge for the v2 top floorplan)
  v2/10  2770 x 2680   7.42   0.2, 10 iterations    usage 67.8 %, 64,593 overflowed gcells, 24.10 m
                                                    (launched as a 20 um-halo test; the halo override
                                                    did not reach RTLMP, so this is v2 with 10 instead
                                                    of 30 congestion iterations)
  L      3400 x 3300   11.22  met1 0.7, others 0.2  still overflowing after 2 x 30 extra iterations; stopped
  z99    3200 x 3100   9.92   0.2, 10 iterations    usage 57.5 % (m1 61.4, m2 67.7, m3 43.0, m4 49.1),
                                                    17,997 overflowed gcells, 28.67 m wire
  z112   3400 x 3300   11.22  0.2, 10 iterations    usage 51.4 % (m1 58.4, m2 59.3, m3 38.7, m4 36.9),
                                                    177 overflowed gcells, 29.08 m wire
  => the CPU block needs ~11 mm^2 to global-route without overflow;
     the Caravel die can give it at most ~7.4 mm^2 (with the crypto
     block at ~2.2 mm^2 and a ~0.5 mm^2 glue column).
  After CTS (r1): design area 5.08 mm^2 (69 % of the block incl.
  macros); setup WNS -0.06 ns at 40 ns (reg-to-reg, before routing).
  The CPU's wire demand is the problem: 28.3 m of global route for
  1.90 mm^2 of cells (crypto: 8.9 m for 1.07 mm^2).

== Top level, v1 dry run (provisional block abstracts from 4_cts)
  synth/floorplan/PDN/place/CTS complete: block power pins connect
  (389 PDN-0110 warnings, all on the OpenRAM macros' own 0.49 um met4
  supply columns that the abstract also lists; the 1.6 um straps over
  them get via4). GRT: 79,305 overflowed gcells (met1 34,105, met2
  39,974) - the ~400 nets from the CPU's 2900 um top edge had to reach
  die pins sideways through a 90 um channel, and GRT spread them over
  both blocks where only met5 is free. Fixed by the v2 floorplan.

== Top level, v2 dry run (provisional block abstracts from the v2 blocks' 4_cts)
  synth 22 s; floorplan/PDN/place/CTS complete. GRT: usage 24.2 %,
  3,536 overflowed gcells (79,305 in v1), the rest over the CPU block's
  left part (x 100-500, y 1750-2750) where only met5 is free.
  DRT: 20,045 / 8,262 / 7,269 / 3,558 / 2,714 / 2,525 / 2,358 / 2,282 /
  2,266 / 2,228 / 2,159 / 2,116 / 2,103 / 1,690 / 1,111 / 1,009
  (iteration 15; stopped, the top-level flow works end to end).

== Crypto block v3 (2770 x 780, bottom-edge pins; final v2 floorplan slot)
  The first v2 shape (2770 x 810, pins on the 810 um left edge) put the
  AXI register/read-mux logic at one end of the block: HPWL +12 %,
  global-route wire 11.00 m vs 8.90 m, 7,873 overflowed gcells at
  74.9 % usage. With the pins on the bottom edge (v3): 0 overflow,
  68.3 % usage, 9.43 m.
  DRT (11 threads, restarted from the GRT result; earlier 2- and
  5-thread attempts were stopped for speed): 329,901 / 258,127 / 242,189 /
  111,676 / 62,001 / 40,519 / 30,200 / 24,469 / 18,713 (iteration 8,
  ~10 min per iteration), still falling ~20 % per iteration. Not
  finished when this log was written: the run (container
  pnr-hier-crypto-v3c, target generate_abstract) was left going on the
  host and records its end in ~/sigil-hier/build/STATUS; its route
  DRC / antenna / timing are therefore not reported here.

== Wall times (host, UTC 2026-10-22 01:35-15:05, ~13.5 h)
  synthesis: crypto 249 s, CPU 2,904 s, top 22 s
  crypto r1 (slot, to DRT iteration 1) 4,407 s; crypto-s (DRT resumed,
    iterations 0-8, 3 threads) 15,768 s; d35/d42 3,907 s; m42 1,967 s;
    h30 3,613 s; u30 4,923 s; v3 DRT to iteration 8 (11 threads) ~2.3 h
  CPU r1 (to GRT) 5,706 s; cpu-s (GRT stage only) 11,315 s; cpu-v2
    (floorplan..GRT) 9,598 s; L 5,550 s; v2/10 3,771 s; z99/z112 3,583 s
  top dry runs: v1 1,300 s (to GRT); v2 1,263 s (GRT + 15 DRT iterations)
  aes calibration 4,909 s (1 thread)
  Global routing of the CPU block is the slow step: FastRoute's
  congestion iterations are single-threaded, and with overflow the
  GRT stage (with repair_design, repair_timing and the incremental
  re-routes) took 2.5-3+ h.

== Reproduce (ORFS image above; design dirs mounted as in the flat run,
   plus flow/designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier at designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier)
  make DESIGN_CONFIG=designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/sigil_crypto_top/config.mk FLOW_VARIANT=v2 generate_abstract
  make DESIGN_CONFIG=designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/sigil_cpu/config.mk FLOW_VARIANT=v2 generate_abstract
  SIGIL_TOP_SYNTH=1 make DESIGN_CONFIG=designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/config.mk FLOW_VARIANT=v2 synth
  make DESIGN_CONFIG=designs/sky130hd/sigil_hier/config.mk FLOW_VARIANT=v2 finish
  (block FLOW_VARIANT must match the top's: the top reads
  results/sky130hd/sigil_hier_<block>/$(FLOW_VARIANT)/.) Sizing runs
  override DIE_AREA/CORE_AREA on the make command line.

== Host disk
  The host disk filled up once during these runs (0 bytes free while
  copying a CPU variant); superseded variant results were deleted, the
  running jobs were unaffected (they were in GRT/DRT computation).
